1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,179
Everything up to this point has been building toward those twenty minutes of actual

2
00:00:05,179 --> 00:00:05,645
writing.

3
00:00:05,645 --> 00:00:08,205
Open A01 from last lecture and follow along.

4
00:00:08,205 --> 00:00:10,881
What does a complete draft actually mean here?

5
00:00:10,881 --> 00:00:12,277
Not a polished document.

6
00:00:12,277 --> 00:00:16,815
Every section has something in it, even if some of those somethings are rough.

7
00:00:16,815 --> 00:00:20,132
A complete rough draft is a document people can react to.

8
00:00:20,132 --> 00:00:22,983
A blank section is just a gap nobody's found yet.

9
00:00:22,983 --> 00:00:26,939
Choosing the right feature matters more than people expect going in.

10
00:00:26,939 --> 00:00:31,826
Pick something hypothetical, or something so simple it barely has any real risk, and

11
00:00:31,826 --> 00:00:36,481
the workshop feels easy and teaches you almost nothing, because none of the hard

12
00:00:36,481 --> 00:00:38,866
sections have anything real to grip onto.

13
00:00:38,866 --> 00:00:43,463
Pick the feature you're actually a little worried about, the one with a genuine

14
00:00:43,463 --> 00:00:48,175
failure mode you haven't fully named yet, and every section in this template will

15
00:00:48,175 --> 00:00:49,048
earn its place.

16
00:00:49,598 --> 00:00:54,584
Notice acceptance criteria gets the most time on this clock, five minutes, almost a

17
00:00:54,584 --> 00:00:56,386
quarter of the whole workshop.

18
00:00:56,386 --> 00:00:57,767
That's not an accident.

19
00:00:57,767 --> 00:01:02,632
Lecture 3.7 already told you why: get that sentence right and everything below it

20
00:01:02,632 --> 00:01:06,657
gets easier to write, because you already know what you're proving.

21
00:01:06,657 --> 00:01:10,561
Everything after that section should move faster than you expect.

22
00:01:10,561 --> 00:01:15,306
Once the threshold exists, the dataset and rubric sections are mostly restating

23
00:01:15,306 --> 00:01:20,352
decisions you've already made, and the failure taxonomy is just naming the ways this

24
00:01:20,352 --> 00:01:22,334
specific feature actually breaks.

25
00:01:22,334 --> 00:01:27,320
If a later section is taking longer than the acceptance criteria did, that's a sign

26
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:30,383
the acceptance criteria wasn't specific enough yet.

27
00:01:30,383 --> 00:01:33,146
Treat these numbers as a pace, not a deadline.

28
00:01:33,146 --> 00:01:38,192
Twenty minutes is enough time to prove to yourself that a complete draft is possible

29
00:01:38,192 --> 00:01:40,715
in one sitting, which is the actual point.

30
00:01:40,715 --> 00:01:45,700
If you're at minute twenty-five and still finishing the last section, that's a fine

31
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:46,181
outcome.

32
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:51,166
Stopping at minute twelve with three sections untouched, because the first two felt

33
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:55,911
too important to leave imperfect, is the outcome this pace is built to prevent.

34
00:01:56,461 --> 00:01:57,910
Recognise this sentence.

35
00:01:57,910 --> 00:02:02,859
It's the exact rewrite from earlier in this section, and that's deliberately not a

36
00:02:02,859 --> 00:02:03,583
new example.

37
00:02:03,583 --> 00:02:08,291
This is what a real twenty-minute session actually produces when it works: one

38
00:02:08,291 --> 00:02:12,516
specific sentence, four parts, nothing left to a reader's imagination.

39
00:02:12,516 --> 00:02:17,586
If what you've written in the last few minutes doesn't look roughly like this shape,

40
00:02:17,586 --> 00:02:22,414
a dataset, a rubric, a percentage, and a floor, that's worth noticing now, while

41
00:02:22,414 --> 00:02:27,303
you're still in the section, rather than three sections later when it's harder to

42
00:02:27,303 --> 00:02:27,544
fix.

43
00:02:27,544 --> 00:02:32,373
It's worth being honest about what this sentence doesn't need to be: elegant, or

44
00:02:32,373 --> 00:02:33,037
even final.

45
00:02:33,037 --> 00:02:37,865
It needs to be specific enough that changing your mind about it later would be a

46
00:02:37,865 --> 00:02:39,797
visible edit, not a quiet drift.

47
00:02:39,797 --> 00:02:41,969
Fifty cases can become seventy-five.

48
00:02:41,969 --> 00:02:44,263
Ninety percent can become ninety-five.

49
00:02:44,263 --> 00:02:48,910
Both of those are real conversations a document like this one makes possible.

50
00:02:48,910 --> 00:02:53,920
A vague sentence never gets edited, because there was never anything precise enough

51
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:55,308
in it to disagree with.

52
00:02:55,858 --> 00:03:00,484
Say the honest caveat, because it's the thing that actually derails most people's

53
00:03:00,484 --> 00:03:03,682
twenty minutes, and it's rarely the acceptance criteria.

54
00:03:03,682 --> 00:03:08,365
It's usually the rubric, or the failure taxonomy, the sections that feel like they

55
00:03:08,365 --> 00:03:10,649
need to be right before you can move on.

56
00:03:10,649 --> 00:03:11,278
They don't.

57
00:03:11,278 --> 00:03:15,675
Write what you know, mark the rest "TBD, owner assigned by Friday," the exact

58
00:03:15,675 --> 00:03:19,330
honest-gap move from the golden dataset lecture, and keep going.

59
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:22,757
A stalled section costs you the entire rest of the document.

60
00:03:22,757 --> 00:03:25,155
A named gap costs you nothing but honesty.

61
00:03:25,155 --> 00:03:29,495
Notice this is the same instinct fighting you here as the one that built the

62
00:03:29,495 --> 00:03:34,121
untestable line all the way back in lecture one: the urge to write something that

63
00:03:34,121 --> 00:03:37,491
sounds finished rather than something that's actually true.

64
00:03:37,491 --> 00:03:42,117
A dated TBD looks less finished than a vague sentence that quietly means nothing.

65
00:03:42,117 --> 00:03:46,914
It's the better kind of unfinished, because everyone reading it knows exactly what's

66
00:03:46,914 --> 00:03:47,713
still missing.

67
00:03:48,263 --> 00:03:52,149
That's the bar for today, and it's lower than perfect on purpose.

68
00:03:52,149 --> 00:03:57,051
A complete draft with three honest placeholders is something a colleague can react

69
00:03:57,051 --> 00:03:58,127
to this afternoon.

70
00:03:58,127 --> 00:04:02,910
A perfect acceptance criteria section sitting above four blank ones is something

71
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:07,632
nobody can react to at all, because there's nothing there yet to disagree with.

72
00:04:07,632 --> 00:04:11,279
If you finish with time left, don't polish everything evenly.

73
00:04:11,279 --> 00:04:16,300
Go back to the one sentence this whole section has been about, and ask whether you'd

74
00:04:16,300 --> 00:04:18,572
actually defend it in a launch review.

75
00:04:19,122 --> 00:04:20,809
One thing before you move on.

76
00:04:20,809 --> 00:04:25,639
Get every section touched, gaps marked with a date and an owner instead of silence,

77
00:04:25,639 --> 00:04:29,362
and put it in front of one real colleague before the day is out.

78
00:04:29,362 --> 00:04:32,097
Ask them specifically what they'd push back on.

79
00:04:32,097 --> 00:04:36,635
That answer will teach you more about this section than another lecture would.

80
00:04:36,635 --> 00:04:41,407
Expect them to push hardest on the acceptance criteria, the same section that took

81
00:04:41,407 --> 00:04:43,268
the most of your twenty minutes.

82
00:04:43,268 --> 00:04:44,549
That's not a bad sign.

83
00:04:44,549 --> 00:04:49,261
It means the document is finally specific enough for someone to disagree with it,

84
00:04:49,261 --> 00:04:52,753
which is the entire difference this section set out to make.

85
00:04:52,753 --> 00:04:57,582
Next lecture closes out Section three: what you've actually built, and how directly

86
00:04:57,582 --> 00:05:01,015
it plugs into the eval methodology waiting in Section four.
